Diagnosis:
The test case `testUnsafeDeSerialization()` creates instances of `MultiValueMap` using the static factory method `MultiValueMap.multiValueMap(Map, Class)`. This method internally creates a `MultiValueMap` instance passing the given underlying map and a `ReflectionFactory` created with the provided class.

From the code snippet of `multiValueMap`:
```java
public static <K, V, C extends Collection<V>> MultiValueMap<K, V> multiValueMap(final Map<K, ? super C> map,
                                                                             final Class<C> collectionClass) {
    return new MultiValueMap<K, V>(map, new ReflectionFactory<C>(collectionClass));
}
```
We see that it uses `ReflectionFactory` which probably uses reflection to instantiate collection instances during operation or deserialization.

The constructor of `MultiValueMap` takes a `Factory<C>` (here `ReflectionFactory<C>`) and assigns it to a field after validating it is not null:
```java
protected <C extends Collection<V>> MultiValueMap(final Map<K, ? super C> map,
                                                  final Factory<C> collectionFactory) {
    super((Map<K, Object>) map);
    if (collectionFactory == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("The factory must not be null");
    }
    this.collectionFactory = collectionFactory;
}
```

The test serializes and deserializes a `MultiValueMap` that uses `ArrayList.class` as the collection class (safe), then tries to do the same with `String.class` (which is suspicious as String is not a collection).

On deserialization of the second map with `String.class` as collectionClass, the test expects an `UnsupportedOperationException` but fails because such exception is not thrown in the current implementation.

The bug report mentions an "Arbitrary remote code execution with InvokerTransformer" vulnerability that can be triggered because certain classes used in deserialized collections (like `ReflectionFactory` with untrusted class types) allow the execution of arbitrary Java code during deserialization.

In conclusion:
- The deserialization logic currently allows instantiating any `Class<C>` through `ReflectionFactory`.
- If an unsafe class (like String.class) is used, it can lead to remote code execution.
- The test expects unsafe classes to be detected and rejected during deserialization by throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`.
- The current `MultiValueMap` or `ReflectionFactory` code does not properly validate or guard against unsafe class types when deserializing, allowing exploitation.

The root cause of the bug is the lack of validation or protection against unsafe class types used for collection instantiation during deserialization in `MultiValueMap` or the factory it uses (`ReflectionFactory`). This allows malicious payloads to be passed to the deserialization process, resulting in remote code execution vulnerabilities.

Next steps will be to check the `ReflectionFactory` implementation and deserialization handling to confirm missing protections.

